#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119586 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection"}] :question/id #uuid "b36a943c-f00a-5436-9256-0725a0008fdb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5410" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/assets/c0a1d1860f5be98c6606f191fd26b0f799bc182b7b4e7fdca542d693290f11e4.png)" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form. We see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $2$nd columns. This tells us that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ forms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* two vectors. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/assets/268ea5cffffb0765f5c4bbacff7f3253ad4243e0e1ffa77d3a8a5a7f794d62ba.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119587 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "004c4177-ca0e-586c-962a-1220b4b64f47" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span Using Row Reduction"}] :question/id #uuid "ef028d89-e645-5ef3-a881-092292e2a271" :question/math-academy-id "e-5411" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/assets/668c8e17567f5635f3d39c98dced5c77c60a287cac8a92d29db2e8afefe94cec.png)" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -15 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has one pivot columns: the $1$st column. This tells us that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1 \\}$ forms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of one vector, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* one vector. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/assets/4196e42d01f879b24974be9c0d1bc456c4894cb19b832efab46f3cdb8a28b644.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e3f340c-ed19-50cb-8196-45eb2e65caad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350414} {:answer/id #uuid "2d770e5d-5fe9-58f8-83a8-345cd94e0423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350415} {:answer/id #uuid "dec7751d-4aa9-522e-ab3f-2242cdaf5500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350416} {:answer/id #uuid "44b2ed1d-0846-5bf2-8713-bfb3b98c2be7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350417} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4c6d3c-277f-5519-b973-23ed4c58062b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dec7751d-4aa9-522e-ab3f-2242cdaf5500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350416} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6d0145b-6384-5187-b500-9c183f001d2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4bbe3484-6ec6-5a37-a5e3-f6675f0e9f72" :question/math-academy-id "q-159784" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=4$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$\n\n- Statement II is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3$ (not $4$).\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=2$ (not $3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "004c4177-ca0e-586c-962a-1220b4b64f47" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "860066dc-9678-5a9d-bd86-3b1b4c18b1e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118258} {:answer/id #uuid "8ffc6e4a-31a3-509a-b8b1-cf21e52432c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118259} {:answer/id #uuid "9942e06c-9691-5e59-8c96-1e6d1e996cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118260} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0986b8-8d54-5fee-aee8-e21bc2520f0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118261} {:answer/id #uuid "36bf313b-75f8-5376-971e-919c2d325bb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9942e06c-9691-5e59-8c96-1e6d1e996cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118260} :answer-field/id #uuid "01959ec0-9d78-5ae7-8581-d050edc64ee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5704a84d-0989-5302-98de-8c50b889e0c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-34854" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]})=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "004c4177-ca0e-586c-962a-1220b4b64f47" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d1004ba-640e-53a1-9fb2-2981c375e44e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350421} {:answer/id #uuid "790328e1-6115-5e73-845a-a7f16da7181f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350422} {:answer/id #uuid "093dc2f6-84c2-5355-a543-332e1beb09b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350423} {:answer/id #uuid "c7537fcb-2e70-5226-8f23-eac14afc5ea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350424} {:answer/id #uuid "e83b6320-0865-56f0-ab2c-3b555301ed02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "093dc2f6-84c2-5355-a543-332e1beb09b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350423} :answer-field/id #uuid "195293fd-16ff-549f-baec-d75faf4d0827" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2cc5280e-8de7-5945-a4e2-7c56b5047caa" :question/math-academy-id "q-35292" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 22 & -44 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{22}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 22 & -44 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "004c4177-ca0e-586c-962a-1220b4b64f47" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d825f059-1da7-5a39-bb67-395e2565dada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350428} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb025b6-7556-51dc-86e4-306fe3d06d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350429} {:answer/id #uuid "42762e24-600e-548d-a09d-ef1cd7430f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350430} {:answer/id #uuid "96d94e34-8588-50c0-a11f-da94eae7460e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350431} {:answer/id #uuid "90d9e8c6-9efb-5ef2-be0f-ae22ae8716d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5eb025b6-7556-51dc-86e4-306fe3d06d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350429} :answer-field/id #uuid "0848333f-e4d1-5bd9-ba0d-3158a7d71879" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0cd93409-2ec3-5842-bb55-b8071c12a478" :question/math-academy-id "q-35297" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{3}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "004c4177-ca0e-586c-962a-1220b4b64f47" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a38ab039-c6eb-5218-b8a7-44a5b762b502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350435} {:answer/id #uuid "452a7eb0-d4fb-58c5-bf83-d8a70de3030c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350436} {:answer/id #uuid "a083a7d5-a3e1-5896-8b92-7a8b6e4d4025" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350437} {:answer/id #uuid "997e8260-7d34-5077-a6a6-4f6b53f980d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350438} {:answer/id #uuid "acfe5f33-c92d-51fa-bebc-e8aad46184ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a083a7d5-a3e1-5896-8b92-7a8b6e4d4025" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350437} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e6b29c7-52c0-5db8-9183-dc0d9b9b1756" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93b94338-89dd-53cb-a299-c2b63ff66b73" :question/math-academy-id "q-35298" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]})=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "004c4177-ca0e-586c-962a-1220b4b64f47" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3287438-77ac-5b49-8e63-90927e217597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350442} {:answer/id #uuid "2c02527d-4912-542c-82ab-6ed6c8796378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350443} {:answer/id #uuid "deebd14b-3cd4-5166-a2dc-cb94211e1313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350444} {:answer/id #uuid "3028e8d7-38a8-5173-a15e-1547db7dffd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350445} {:answer/id #uuid "e680489a-d778-5a90-b6de-ca6f57db6d1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "deebd14b-3cd4-5166-a2dc-cb94211e1313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350444} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e71262d-4c70-5133-acec-9ea1f1763548" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "74865fe2-0ffb-5a64-9ede-829c8967c316" :question/math-academy-id "q-35302" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 5 & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]})=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d99185df-501c-5fc0-ad07-8396c93a532b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350449} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4b0b3b-68c7-590b-a5d0-bb6294ff3d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350450} {:answer/id #uuid "cb5cf2a1-75ad-5b03-a572-