vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea8e400c-4cb8-5b95-b5d2-de18dac61c50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350463} {:answer/id #uuid "4533b8b1-0e73-573d-88b1-1e1176acfb5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350464} {:answer/id #uuid "7803d8b1-9764-5187-9445-246a598082b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350465} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9f7ebd-52bf-5b0c-889f-0bd17c6af181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350466} {:answer/id #uuid "38c4fd6d-a531-57d7-b0b0-876303d4220a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e9f7ebd-52bf-5b0c-889f-0bd17c6af181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350466} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d39f96b-cb50-5b97-8744-a6dfd6d30bcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12cb29bf-f056-51b7-aa00-a244ffe6e6cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-79322" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $4$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $4$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc5b635d-71dd-594e-9fc1-60d35844414d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350470} {:answer/id #uuid "9f3dfa4e-7a61-55f2-b5f8-f68003697e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350471} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba19d77-fd3a-5d71-934b-f082f41e05f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350472} {:answer/id #uuid "0027c121-006e-599d-8e5b-6af6f356fe95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350473} {:answer/id #uuid "18e2108f-46f0-5959-b16f-37710999436d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc5b635d-71dd-594e-9fc1-60d35844414d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350470} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b0023e2-cac4-56ad-9a03-7b5ce3a018b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77009195-0b55-53e1-b37a-b9c64dc878fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-79323" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}})=3$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and these are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}})=3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the given span and this basis contains $3$ vectors, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
